<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/brain-anatomy-tour">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You know from the foundational lesson what a single neuron is and how it signals. This lesson is
    the building: roughly <strong>86 billion neurons</strong> (plus a comparable number of glia)
    arranged in a structure so orderly that damage to a specific region produces a specific deficit —
    and that one-to-one correspondence is how the map was first drawn. Before brain imaging existed,
    the anatomy came from injuries and diseases: a patient loses speech, and an autopsy finds the
    lesion in a particular patch of cortex; another loses the ability to form new memories, and the
    lesion sits in the hippocampus. The brain's anatomy is therefore functional anatomy — every
    structure on the tour earns its place by what goes wrong without it. We will climb through the
    whole organ: the outer cortex and its four lobes, the deep nuclei below it, the life-support
    machinery at the base, and the hemispheric split that generated one of neuroscience's most
    persistent myths.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the regions, their jobs, and the famous injuries that mapped them" widget="equationStep" config={anatomyCfg} />

  <h2>Three scales: system, sheet, map</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the frame. The nervous system divides into the <strong>central</strong> nervous system
    (brain and spinal cord) and the <strong>peripheral</strong> nervous system (the nerves reaching
    out to body and organs — motor commands out, sensory reports in, with the autonomic division
    running the organs largely outside awareness). The brain itself is a folded sheet: the
    <strong>cortex</strong> is a layer only 2–4 mm thick, but spread out it covers roughly 2,500 cm²
    — about a large dinner plate — and the wrinkles (gyri and sulci) pack that sheet into a skull
    that could otherwise hold a third of it. About 16 billion of the brain's neurons live in the
    cortex; the remaining ~70 billion crowd the small, densely packed <strong>cerebellum</strong> at
    the back — a fact that surprises most people and that we will return to. Development sets the
    sheet's geography before birth, and the same regions occupy the same relative positions in
    everyone, which is why a textbook map works at all. Within the cortex, Korbinian Brodmann's 1909
    atlas — drawn purely from how cells stack in each patch — distinguished dozens of areas, and the
    number-mapped functions turned out to respect those borders to a remarkable degree (Brodmann
    area 17 is still primary visual cortex; area 4 is still primary motor cortex).
  </p>

  <h2>The four lobes</h2>
  <p>
    The cortex is conventionally divided into four lobes per hemisphere, each anchored to a primary
    sensory or motor role and wrapped in association cortex that does the integrating.
    <strong>Occipital</strong>, at the back, is vision: primary visual cortex receives the retina's
    traffic via the thalamus, and damage here produces <em>cortical blindness</em> — eyes intact,
    nerves intact, but no conscious sight. <strong>Parietal</strong>, on top, is touch and spatial
    sense: primary somatosensory cortex receives the body map, and the surrounding association areas
    merge touch, position and vision into a model of where "here" is; a right-parietal stroke can
    produce <em>neglect</em> — a patient who eats only the food on the right half of the plate and
    insists the left arm belongs to someone else, revealing how much of "the world" is a cortical
    construction. <strong>Temporal</strong>, behind the ears, is hearing, language comprehension and
    memory (the hippocampus tucks inside it): Wernicke's area, typically in the dominant temporal
    lobe, is where fluent but meaningless speech is produced and comprehension fails when damaged —
    the patient speaks easily and says nothing. <strong>Frontal</strong>, the largest lobe, is
    planning, decision-making, working memory, inhibition and voluntary movement. Its rearmost strip,
    primary motor cortex, executes movement commands; everything in front does the deciding about
    what to execute. The classic evidence is Phineas Gage: in 1848 an iron tamping rod shot through
    his left frontal lobe in a railway accident; he survived — could walk, talk, remember — but his
    associates reported that "Gage was no longer Gage," a capable man made impulsive and socially
    inappropriate. Modern reconstructions of his skull confirm the orbitofrontal and medial frontal
    damage, and the case founded the principle that the frontal lobes carry not just plans but the
    <em>value weightings</em> plans depend on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two famous maps live on the strip between frontal and parietal. <strong>Primary motor cortex</strong>
    (frontal) and <strong>primary somatosensory cortex</strong> (parietal) each contain a distorted
    body map — the <em>homunculus</em> — in which body parts are drawn in proportion to cortical
    real estate, not size: the hand and lips monopolise huge stretches, the trunk almost none. The
    distortion is honest engineering — lips and fingers need fine discrimination and fine control,
    backs do not — and the map's plasticity (a violinist's finger territory expands) is one of the
    strongest experimental confirmations that "use it or lose it" is literally cortical.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the lobes are not isolated modules doing one job each. Every
  complex behaviour — reading a sentence, recognising a face — runs a distributed network across
  lobes; the lobe labels name the primary entry points and the dominant contribution, not exclusive
  ownership.</p>

  <h2>Below the cortex: the deep structures</h2>
  <p>
    Under the cortical sheet sit the nuclei that make the cortex usable. The <strong>thalamus</strong>
    is the relay hub: nearly all sensory traffic bound for cortex stops here for gating — the reason
    you sleep through a ticking clock but wake at your name — and it also loops signals back to
    cortex in the cortico-thalamic circuits that maintain consciousness itself. The
    <strong>hippocampus</strong> converts experience into long-term memory; Patient H.M., whose
    medial temporal lobes (hippocampi included) were resected in 1953 to treat epilepsy, could
    remember his childhood and hold a thought, but could not form a single new conscious memory for
    the remaining 55 years of his life — and yet he improved at mirror-drawing day by day while
    denying ever having done the task, which revealed memory as multiple systems (a
    <em>declarative</em> system that H.M. lost, and procedural systems that he kept). The
    <strong>amygdala</strong>, curled against the hippocampus, assigns emotional salience — fear
    conditioning, threat detection, the memory-boosting punch of emotionally charged events; a
    patient with bilateral amygdala damage (S.M.) could not recognise fear in faces and was
    effectively fearless, walking through situations most people find terrifying. The <strong>basal
    ganglia</strong> — striatum, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus — are the gating-and-selection
    system between cortex and movement (and, less obviously, between options and action generally):
    their degeneration produces Parkinson's disease (too little movement) and Huntington's disease
    (too much). And the <strong>cerebellum</strong> at the back is the precision-timing machine: it
    calibrates movements against their sensory consequences, holds the models that let you catch a
    ball without solving the physics, and its neurons — roughly 69 billion, mostly tiny granule
    cells — make up the majority of the brain's total neuron count.
  </p>

  <h2>The life-support level</h2>
  <p>
    Below all of this sits the <strong>brainstem</strong> — midbrain, pons, medulla — the trunk where
    every cable between brain and body passes and where the non-negotiable housekeeping runs:
    breathing rhythm, heart rate, blood pressure, swallowing, arousal and sleep-wake switching.
    Damage here is uniquely dangerous precisely because it is not optional tissue; patients can
    survive with lost cortex but not lost brainstem, and "brain death" is legally and clinically a
    brainstem determination. The brain's plumbing also deserves a look: the ventricles — hollow
    chambers in the brain's core — produce about 500 mL of cerebrospinal fluid a day (the pool
    surrounding brain and cord holds about 150 mL, renewed several times daily), cushioning the
    ~1.4 kg organ to an effective weight of a few tens of grams and circulating waste; the meninges
    (dura, arachnoid, pia) wrap the whole structure; and the <strong>blood–brain barrier</strong> —
    tight junctions in the capillaries feeding brain tissue — admits oxygen and glucose while
    excluding most molecules, which is why designing drugs for the brain (the neurology lesson's
    recurring headache) is hard, and why the brain, 2% of body mass, commands about 20% of the
    body's energy budget: it never stops computing, even asleep.
  </p>

  <h2>Two hemispheres, one brain</h2>
  <p>
    Each hemisphere controls the opposite body side (the corticospinal tract crosses at the
    brainstem), reads the opposite visual field, and senses the opposite body half — a design
    peculiar to the nervous system and worth simply accepting. Lateralisation beyond that is real
    but modest: language lives in the left hemisphere in about 95% of right-handers (Broca's 1861
    patient "Tan," who could say almost nothing after left-frontal damage but understood speech, and
    Wernicke's 1874 opposite case, anchored the map within a decade of each other), while faces,
    spatial processing and prosody lean right. The myth — "logical left brain, creative right
    brain" — oversells this into personality types, and the evidence against it is direct: the
    <strong>corpus callosum</strong>, a bundle of some 200 million fibres, keeps the hemispheres in
    constant conversation, and ordinary cognition is thoroughly integrated. The dramatic exception
    is instructive: in split-brain patients — callosum severed to stop intractable epilepsy — Sperry
    and Gazzaniga showed that the two hemispheres can literally work independently (an object shown
    only to the right hemisphere can be picked out by the left hand, while the speaking left
    hemisphere denies seeing it), work that earned Sperry the 1981 Nobel Prize. The split-brain
    results prove specialisation is real — and simultaneously prove the myth wrong, because only in
    surgically severed brains does any of the "two minds" story appear at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Several refinements keep the tour honest. <strong>The map has individual variation</strong>:
    textbook locations are averages — the exact cortical placement of language areas varies by
    centimetres between people (which is why neurosurgeons map eloquent cortex per patient before
    resecting), and handedness shifts the lateralisation statistics. <strong>The triune-brain story
    is retired</strong>: the tidy "reptilian brain, limbic system, neocortex" layering of popular
    psychology is not how evolution built the organ — structures overlapped, co-opted and retooled
    across lineages, and modern comparative work rejects the layer metaphor even while the
    "limbic" shorthand survives for clinical convenience. <strong>White matter is half the
    story</strong>: the cortex gets the glory, but the brain's long-range wiring — myelinated axon
    tracts carrying traffic between regions — occupies similar volume, and much of what degenerates
    in ageing and traumatic brain injury is connectivity, not cell bodies; diffusion MRI now images
    these tracts living, and the emerging "connectome" describes the brain as a network whose
    hub-and-module statistics predict function better than any regional list. <strong>Injury
    effects depend on network, not address</strong>: modern lesion-mapping (re-analysing Gage
    digitally, or tracing specific disconnection syndromes) shows the same cortical patch damaged in
    different patients can yield different deficits when the damaged <em>connections</em> differ —
    the unit of function is increasingly the circuit. And <strong>plasticity is the fourth
    dimension</strong> of the map: homunculus territories expand and contract with use, phantom-limb
    pain reflects cortical remapping after amputation, and rehabilitation works partly by recruiting
    adjacent cortex. The tour, in short, describes a scaffold — but the scaffold's rooms are
    re-carpeted constantly, which is precisely why this organ can learn.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; Azevedo et al. on neuron counts (2009); Sperry (1981) Nobel lecture; van Horn et al. on Gage (2012); MacLean's triune model (with modern caveats).</p>
